.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library is the controller side of a DescriptorQueue, the
.Xr dq 4
device.
A controller opens a queue, mints descriptors that present an identity of
its choosing, reads the events the kernel posts for operations on those
descriptors and answers them by id.
Every function is a thin wrapper over the
.Xr ioctl 2
and
.Xr read 2
interface of
.In dev/dq/dq.h ;
the kernel's errnos pass through unchanged and are listed below per
function.
Functions return 0 on success and -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure, except those documented to return a descriptor, a count or
a pointer.
The interface is LP64 only.
.Ss Queue
.Fn dq_open
opens
.Pa /dev/dq
with
.Dv O_RDWR | O_CLOEXEC | O_CLOFORK
and returns the queue descriptor.
One open is one queue.
The queue is closed on fork so that a child the controller forks inherits
the minted descriptors and not the queue; a process whose descriptor table
holds the queue is refused operations on the queue's objects with
.Er EDEADLK
.Pq see Xr dq 4 .
Opening fails with
.Er ENOENT
when the device is not configured,
.Er EPERM
or
.Er EACCES
without the
.Dv PRIV_DRIVER
privilege, and
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
from a 32-bit process.
.Pp
.Fn dq_get_stats
fills
.Fa st
with the
.Vt struct dq_stats
of the queue, or of the queue an observer descriptor
.Fa fd
watches.
.Pp
.Fn dq_set_queue_depth
lowers the number of unread events the queue holds;
.Fa depth
must be between 1 and
.Va kern.dq.queue_depth
or the call fails with
.Er EINVAL .
A full queue fails the requester's operation with
.Er EIO ,
counts a drop and leaves a gap in
.Va de_seq .
.Ss Identity and policy
.Fn dq_policy_default
fills
.Fa pol
with the policy stage 1a of
.Xr dq 4
accepts:
.Va dp_cache
.Dv DQ_CACHE_NONE
(no page cache, which is stage 1b; whether
.Xr fstat 2
posts is decided by
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR
alone),
.Va dp_write ,
.Va dp_exec ,
.Va dp_mmap_shared_write
and
.Va dp_ioctl
.Dv DQ_DENY ,
.Va dp_lock
.Dv DQ_EVENT ,
.Va dp_deadline_ms
.Dv DQ_DEADLINE_DEFAULT ,
no
.Va dp_events ,
.Va dp_rights_version
.Dv CAP_RIGHTS_VERSION
and
.Va dp_rights
.Dv CAP_ALL_RIGHTS ,
which is what
.Xr open 2
returns.
A controller narrows the rights or adds
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_GETATTR
and
.Dv DQ_EVENTS_CLOSE
before minting.
.Pp
.Fn dq_ident_from_stat
fills every field of
.Fa ident
from a real file:
.Fa path
is copied as presented (it must be absolute and normalized, as
.Xr realpath 3
returns it, or the mint fails with
.Dv DQ_REASON_PATH ) ,
the
.Vt struct stat
and
.Vt struct statfs
the caller took supply the attributes and the mount, and
.Xr fpathconf 2
is asked on
.Fa fd
for every
.Dv _PC_*
name in
.Va dq_pc_names[] ;
a name the filesystem refuses is left out of
.Va dp_valid
so the served descriptor refuses it with
.Er EINVAL
too.
.Fa fd
may be -1 to present no
.Xr pathconf 2
answers.
.Va di_blksize
is floored at the page size, which the kernel requires.
It fails with
.Er EINVAL
for a null or empty path and
.Er ENAMETOOLONG
for a path of
.Dv DQ_PATH_MAX
bytes or more.
.Ss Objects
.Fn dq_mint_file
mints a
.Dv DQ_KIND_FILE
object presenting
.Fa ident
under
.Fa pol .
On success
.Fa *object
is the object id, never reused on the queue, and
.Fa *fd
the descriptor installed in the caller:
read-only, close-on-exec, limited to
.Va dp_rights ,
and not transferable over
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
.Pq Er ENOTCAPABLE .
Until the resolution stage installs served descriptors in a requester, the
descriptor reaches one by inheritance across
.Xr fork 2 .
The
.Fa reason
pointer, which may be
.Dv NULL ,
is handed to the kernel as
.Va dm_reasonp
(a failing
.Xr ioctl 2
copies its argument back to nobody): on
.Er EINVAL
the kernel stores the refused field there as an
.Vt enum dq_reason
.Pq Xr dq 4 ,
on success
.Dv DQ_REASON_NONE ,
and on any other failure the library has already stored
.Dv DQ_REASON_NONE .
Other errors are
.Er ENOSPC
when the queue holds
.Va kern.dq.max_objects
objects,
.Er ENXIO
when the queue is closing, and those of
.Xr open 2
for the descriptor table.
.Pp
.Fn dq_mint_observer
mints an observer descriptor of the queue and returns it.
It receives a copy of every event and every reply posted on the queue,
with reply data removed and lengths kept;
.Dv DQ_OBS_F_DATA
asks for data bodies where a stage carries them.
The descriptor is readable, pollable, answers
.Dv DQ_IOC_GET_STATS
.Pq Fn dq_get_stats
and nothing else, and is locked close-on-exec, close-on-fork and
non-transferable.
.Er EINVAL
for an unknown flag,
.Er ENXIO
for a closing queue.
.Pp
.Fn dq_revoke
revokes
.Fa object :
every pending and in-flight event of it fails with
.Er EIO ,
.Dv NOTE_REVOKE
fires, later reads answer
.Er EIO ,
and
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr fstatfs 2 ,
.Xr fpathconf 2
and
.Dv F_KINFO
keep answering from the frozen identity.
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown id.
.Pp
.Fn dq_set_ident
replaces the presented identity after validation and fires
.Dv NOTE_ATTRIB
on the object's
.Dv EVFILT_VNODE
knotes.
.Er EINVAL
for an identity the kernel refuses (no reason is returned),
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown object.
.Pp
.Fn dq_set_policy
changes
.Va dp_cache ,
.Va dp_deadline_ms
and
.Va dp_events
of a minted object.
The fields the installed descriptor fixes,
.Va dp_write ,
.Va dp_exec ,
.Va dp_mmap_shared_write ,
.Va dp_lock ,
.Va dp_ioctl
and
.Va dp_rights ,
must equal the minted values or the call fails with
.Er EINVAL ;
so does a policy outside the accepted set.
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown object.
.Ss Events
.Fn dq_read_events
performs one
.Xr read 2
of whole records into
.Fa buf
and stores the byte count in
.Fa *got .
The kernel copies as many whole records as fit, in order, and never splits
one;
.Fa len
below the size of a
.Vt struct dq_event
is
.Er EINVAL ,
a first record that does not fit is
.Er ENOBUFS ,
an empty queue opened or set non-blocking is
.Er EAGAIN ,
a closing queue is
.Er ENXIO ,
and a signal is
.Er EINTR .
A fault in
.Fa buf
after some records were copied returns those and leaves the rest queued.
On an observer descriptor a count of 0 means the watched queue is gone and
drained.
.Pp
.Fn dq_event_first
and
.Fn dq_event_next
walk the records in a buffer of
.Fa len
bytes.
Each returns
.Dv NULL
when no further whole record follows, that is when fewer than a header's
bytes remain or
.Va de_size
is smaller than a header or extends past
.Fa len .
.Fn dq_event_body
returns the body that follows the header and stores its length in
.Fa *bodylen
when that is not
.Dv NULL .
.Pp
.Fn dq_reply
answers the event
.Fa id
with
.Fa error
(0 or an errno) and the body
.Fa data
of
.Fa len
bytes.
For
.Dv DQ_OP_READ
the body is up to the asked length of file bytes and a short answer is
the end of the file; for
.Dv DQ_OP_GETATTR
it is exactly one
.Vt struct dq_ident_file
when
.Fa error
is 0.
Any nonzero
.Fa error
takes an empty body.
.Er ESRCH
means the id is not in flight: unknown, already answered, abandoned by an
interrupted or timed-out requester, or revoked (counted in
.Va ds_late_replies ) .
.Er EINVAL
is returned for a body longer than asked, a body with a nonzero error, a
malformed identity, an errno outside the kernel's range, a length above
.Va kern.dq.max_io ,
or a reply to an event that takes none.
.Er EFAULT
for an unreadable
.Fa data .
.Pp
.Fn dq_dispatch
performs one
.Fn dq_read_events
into a buffer sized for the largest record the kernel can post and calls
.Fa handler
once per record with the header, the body and
.Fa arg .
The handler returns 0 to continue or nonzero to stop.
.Fn dq_dispatch
returns the number of records handed to the handler, or -1 with the errno
of the read.
Records needing no answer
.Pq Dv DQ_OP_CLOSE , Dv DQ_OP_INTERRUPT
carry
.Va de_id
0.
.Ss The dq.observe service
.Fn dq_observe_connect
opens
.Pa /dev/mac_capability ,
connects to the
.Dq dq.observe
service and returns the service descriptor; the device is closed again.
The service admits a credential that may open
.Pa /dev/dq .
.Pp
.Fn dq_observe_list_queues
lists the queues of the system starting at index
.Fa first
into
.Fa buf Ns Bq Fa nbuf
and stores how many were filled in
.Fa *count
and how many exist in
.Fa *total
(either may be
.Dv NULL ) ;
.Fn dq_observe_list_objects
does the same for the objects of
.Fa queue ,
identified by its
.Va ds_queue_id .
A caller pages by advancing
.Fa first .
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown queue,
.Er EPROTO
for a malformed reply.
.Pp
.Fn dq_observe_subscribe
returns a metadata-only observer descriptor of
.Fa queue ,
as
.Fn dq_mint_observer
without
.Dv DQ_OBS_F_DATA
would: the service installs it in the caller itself, rights-limited and
locked like an ioctl-minted observer, and names it in a
.Vt struct dqo_subscribe_reply .
.Er ESRCH
for an unknown queue,
.Er ENXIO
for a closing one,
.Er EPROTO
for a malformed reply.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dq_open ,
.Fn dq_mint_observer ,
.Fn dq_observe_connect
and
.Fn dq_observe_subscribe
return a descriptor, or -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Fn dq_dispatch
returns a record count, or -1.
.Fn dq_event_first ,
.Fn dq_event_next
and
.Fn dq_event_body
return pointers into the caller's buffer.
.Fn dq_policy_default
returns nothing.
The other functions return 0 on success and -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A controller that serves a file's bytes from memory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int q, fd, reason;
uint64_t object;
struct dq_policy pol;
struct dq_ident_file ident;

q = dq_open();
dq_policy_default(&pol);
dq_ident_from_stat(&ident, path, &st, &sfs, realfd);
if (dq_mint_file(q, &ident, &pol, &object, &fd, &reason) == -1)
	err(1, "mint (reason %d)", reason);
/* fd is inherited by a fork(2)ed requester; the queue is not. */
for (;;)
	if (dq_dispatch(q, handler, &served) == -1)
		err(1, "dq_dispatch");
.Ed
.Pp
where the handler answers a read from the buffer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static int
handler(int q, const struct dq_event *ev, const void *body,
    size_t bodylen, void *arg)
{
	const struct dq_read_body *rb;
	struct served *s = arg;
	size_t n;

	switch (ev->de_op) {
	case DQ_OP_READ:
		rb = body;
		n = rb->dr_offset >= s->len ? 0 :
		    MIN(rb->dr_len, s->len - rb->dr_offset);
		return (dq_reply(q, ev->de_id, 0, s->data + rb->dr_offset,
		    n) == -1);
	case DQ_OP_CLOSE:
		s->closed = true;
		return (0);
	default:
		return (0);
	}
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Pa /usr/share/examples/dq/dqserve.c
is a complete controller.
